{"experiments":[  
  {"hypothesis":"Gamma‐band (30–80 Hz) power increase at word onsets is larger in left temporal sensors than right temporal sensors.","experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare evoked gamma‐band power responses to word onsets in left vs right temporal MEG sensors.","steps":"1. Load `channel_names` from a representative MEG file, strip whitespace, and identify temporal sensors by name (left: names starting with 'MEG01'; right: 'MEG05'). 2. For each of the 10 sessions: a) Load MEG data (`data`, `times`, `sample_frequency`). b) Bandpass‐filter the full‐trace of identified left‐ and right‐temporal sensors in the gamma band (30–80 Hz) and compute analytic amplitude via Hilbert transform. c) Load the session’s `events.tsv`, extract `timemeg` for `kind=='word'`. Limit to first 200 events per session. d) For each word onset, epoch the gamma envelope from –0.2 to +0.5 s around onset. Compute mean post‐onset gamma power (0–0.4 s) minus baseline (–0.2–0 s) per sensor. e) Average effect sizes across sensors within left and within right groups to get two per‐session values. 3. Across sessions, perform a paired t‐test comparing left vs right gamma effect sizes. 4. Optionally, compute effect size (Cohen’s d) and run a cluster‐based permutation test on sensor‐level differences.","deliverables":"1. List of left and right temporal sensor indices and names. 2. Per‐session left and right gamma‐power effect sizes. 3. Bar plot with mean ± SEM for left vs right across sessions, annotated with t‐stat and p‐value. 4. Table of sensor‐level paired t‐statistics and p‐values. 5. Written summary of hemispheric asymmetry findings."  
  }},  
  {"hypothesis":"Longer words (more characters) evoke larger M100 (80–120 ms) evoked‐response amplitudes in auditory sensors.","experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether word length (number of letters) predicts the amplitude of the M100 evoked response in bilateral temporal MEG sensors.","steps":"1. From `channel_names`, select bilateral temporal sensors as above. 2. For each session: a) Load MEG data and `events.tsv`. b) Extract `kind=='word'` segments, read `segment` strings, compute word length (# characters). c) For each word onset (`timemeg`), extract raw MEG evoked data (no filtering) from –0.1 to +0.3 s in temporal sensors. d) Baseline‐correct each epoch using –0.1 to 0 s. e) For each epoch and sensor, find the absolute peak amplitude in the 80–120 ms window (M100). f) Average M100 amplitudes across sensors to get one value per word. g) Collect pairs (word length, M100 amplitude) across all sessions. 3. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model predicting M100 amplitude from word length with random intercepts for session. Test fixed‐effect coefficient for word length. 4. Validate model assumptions (residual normality, homoscedasticity).","deliverables":"1. Scatter plot of word length vs M100 amplitude with fitted regression line. 2. Model summary with coefficient estimate, SE, and p‐value. 3. Table of session‐level intercepts. 4. Diagnostic plots for model residuals. 5. Interpretation of length effect on early auditory response."  
  }},  
  {"hypothesis":"Alpha‐band (8–12 Hz) suppression around word onsets is stronger for words at sentence ends than at sentence beginnings.","experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare post‐onset alpha‐power suppression for sentence‐initial versus sentence‐final words.","steps":"1. From each session’s `events.tsv`, select `kind=='word'` rows. Group by `(chapter, sentenceidx)`, and within each group identify `wordidx==1` (sentence start) and `wordidx==max` (sentence end). 2. Load MEG data and identify frontal sensors (e.g. names starting with 'MEG0[23]' for anterior coverage). 3. Bandpass‐filter frontal‐sensor traces in the alpha band (8–12 Hz) and compute analytic amplitude. 4. For each selected event, epoch amplitude from –0.3 to +0.6 s. 5. Compute mean alpha power in baseline (–0.3 to 0 s) and in suppression window (0.1 to 0.4 s); define suppression magnitude = baseline minus post‐onset. 6. Aggregate per‐session suppression magnitudes separately for initial vs final words. 7. Across sessions, run a paired t‐test on the two conditions; correct for multiple comparisons if testing sensors individually. 8. Optionally, run a two‐way repeated‐measures ANOVA with factors Position (initial/end) and Hemisphere (left/right).","deliverables":"1. Counts of sentence‐initial and final word events per session. 2. Time course plots of alpha amplitude (mean ± SEM) for the two conditions. 3. Bar plot of suppression magnitudes with statistics (t, p). 4. ANOVA table if performed. 5. Written interpretation of end‐of‐sentence alpha suppression."  
  }},  
  {"hypothesis":"Vowel‐onset phonemes induce a stronger beta‐band (13–30 Hz) desynchronization than consonant‐onset phonemes.","experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether phoneme‐type (vowel vs consonant) at phoneme onsets modulates beta‐band power desynchronization in temporal sensors.","steps":"1. Define vowel phoneme labels (e.g. 'AA','AE','AH',…) and treat all other phoneme segments as consonants. 2. For each session’s `events.tsv`, extract `kind=='phoneme'`, map `segment` to vowel or consonant, and record `timemeg`. 3. Load MEG data; identify temporal sensors as before. 4. Bandpass‐filter temporal sensors in beta band (13–30 Hz) and compute analytic amplitude. 5. Epoch each phoneme onset from –0.2 to +0.5 s; compute mean beta power in baseline (–0.2 to 0 s) and in desynchronization window (0.05–0.3 s); define desync magnitude = baseline minus post‐onset. 6. Aggregate desync magnitudes per phoneme type and session. 7. Across sessions, perform paired t‐tests comparing vowel vs consonant desync (average across sensors). 8. Perform sensor‐wise tests with FDR correction. 9. Visualize time‐courses and summary bar plots.","deliverables":"1. Counts of vowel and consonant events per session. 2. Grand‐average beta‐power time‐courses for vowels vs consonants. 3. Bar chart of desync magnitudes with t and p. 4. Table of sensors with significant type effects after FDR. 5. Summary of phoneme‐type influence on beta desynchronization."  
  }},  
  {"hypothesis":"Theta‐band (4–7 Hz) event‐related response to phoneme onsets decreases linearly over sessions, indicating habituation.","experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether the magnitude of the theta evoked‐response to phoneme onsets shows a downward trend across the ten recording sessions.","steps":"1. Identify phoneme onsets (`kind=='phoneme'`) and extract `timemeg` for each session. Limit to first 300 phoneme events per session. 2. Load MEG data; select a set of language‐related sensors (e.g. left temporal cluster). 3. Bandpass‐filter in theta band and compute analytic amplitude. 4. Epoch each onset from –0.2 to +0.5 s; compute mean post‐onset theta (0–0.3 s) minus baseline (–0.2–0 s) per epoch and sensor. Average across sensors and events to get one effect size per session. 5. Fit a linear regression of effect size vs session index (1–10). 6. Test slope against zero (t‐test on coefficient). 7. Optionally run nonparametric trend test (Spearman’s rho).","deliverables":"1. Per‐session theta‐effect values. 2. Scatter plot of effect vs session with regression line. 3. Regression summary (slope, p‐value). 4. Spearman correlation result. 5. Interpretation of habituation trend."  
  }},  
  {"hypothesis":"The absolute time alignment error (|timemeg − timeds|) for word events predicts reduction in early evoked‐response amplitude (50–100 ms) across sessions.","experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether larger MEG‐audio timing jitter (difference between `timemeg` and `timeds`) leads to reduced phase‐locked sensory responses.","steps":"1. For each session, load `events.tsv` and for `kind=='word'` compute jitter = |timemeg − timeds|. 2. Load MEG data and identify auditory sensors. 3. For each word onset, epoch raw data from –0.05 to +0.15 s, baseline‐correct (–0.05 to 0 s), and compute peak amplitude in the 50–100 ms window for each sensor. 4. Average peak amplitude across sensors to obtain one response amplitude per event. 5. Across all events (pooling sessions), fit a linear mixed‐effects model predicting peak amplitude from jitter with random intercepts per session. 6. Test whether jitter coefficient is significantly negative.","deliverables":"1. Histogram of jitter values. 2. Scatter plot jitter vs peak amplitude with regression overlay. 3. Model summary with coefficient and p‐value. 4. Random‐effects estimates. 5. Conclusion on alignment effect."  
  }},  
  {"hypothesis":"Sentence‐initial word onsets evoke larger P200 (150–250 ms) responses than sentence‐internal word onsets in frontal MEG sensors.","experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the P200 evoked‐response amplitude for sentence‐initial vs sentence‐internal word onsets.","steps":"1. In each session’s `events.tsv`, extract `kind=='word'` and group by `(chapter, sentenceidx)`. Label events with `wordidx==1` as ‘initial’ and others as ‘internal’. 2. Load MEG data and select frontal sensors (e.g. names starting with 'MEG02', 'MEG03'). 3. Epoch raw data from –0.1 to +0.4 s around each event, baseline‐correct (–0.1 to 0 s). 4. For each epoch and sensor, find peak amplitude in 150–250 ms window (P200). Average across sensors to get one P200 per event. 5. Within each session, compute mean P200 for initial and internal words. 6. Across sessions, run paired t‐test on initial vs internal means. 7. Optionally, compute sensor‐wise paired t‐tests with FDR correction.","deliverables":"1. Counts of initial vs internal events per session. 2. Time‐courses of evoked potentials (mean ± SEM) over frontal sensors for both conditions. 3. Bar chart of P200 amplitudes with t and p. 4. Table of frontal sensors with significant differences (if any). 5. Interpretation of P200 modulation by sentence position."  
  }},  
  {"hypothesis":"Longer word durations (from `duration` field) correlate with sustained gamma‐band (30–80 Hz) power increases in temporal sensors between 100–500 ms post‐onset.","experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether word‐duration predicts the magnitude of sustained gamma‐band power post‐word onset.","steps":"1. From `events.tsv`, for `kind=='word'` extract `duration` and `timemeg` for all sessions. 2. Load MEG data; select temporal sensors. 3. Bandpass‐filter in gamma band and compute analytic amplitude. 4. Epoch each word onset from –0.1 to +0.6 s; baseline‐correct amplitude (–0.1 to 0 s). 5. For each epoch, compute mean gamma power in the sustained window (0.1–0.5 s) and average across sensors. 6. Pool all events across sessions and fit a linear mixed‐effects model predicting sustained gamma power from word duration with session as random effect. 7. Test the fixed‐effect coefficient for duration. 8. Check model assumptions and plot residuals.","deliverables":"1. Scatter plot of word duration vs sustained gamma power with regression line. 2. Mixed‐effects model summary (coefficient, p‐value). 3. Table of session random‐effects. 4. Diagnostic plots. 5. Interpretation of duration effect on sustained gamma activity."  
  }}  
]}
